\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Distinguished generators and the intersection property}

\p{Choose a base \vocabk{flag}{frap-000D} #{\Phi} in a \vocabk{regular}{frap-000G}
\vocabk{abstract polytope}{frap-000D} #{P} of rank #{n}. By [flag freeness](frap-000H), there is a unique
\vocabk{automorphism}{frap-000G} #{\rho_i} taking #{\Phi} to its rank-#{i} neighbor,
for #{0\leq i<n}. The \vocabk{diamond condition}{frap-000D} and uniqueness give
#{\rho_i^2=1}. For #{|i-j|>1}, the two rank changes are independent;
#{\rho_i\rho_j} and #{\rho_j\rho_i} agree on #{\Phi}, hence are equal.
Connectivity shows that the #{\rho_i} reach every \vocabk{flag}{frap-000D}, so they generate
#{\Gamma(P)}. These are the \newvocab{distinguished generators} of
\citet{Section 5, pp. 13--14}{schulte2017regular}.}

\p{For #{I\subseteq\{0,\ldots,n-1\}}, put
#{G_I=\langle\rho_i:i\in I\rangle}. They also satisfy}
##{G_I\cap G_J=G_{I\cap J}.}
\p{This \newvocab{intersection property} is part of the \newvocab{string C-group}
description \citet{Section 5, equation (18)}{schulte2017regular}.
Involution and nonadjacent commutation relations alone do not reconstruct
a polytope; the group-theoretic characterization includes this additional
condition \citet{Section 5, p. 14}{schulte2017regular}.}
